                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 178 litre external storage buffer tank. The boiler has a 5" flue vent &amp; there is a 5" open combustion air supply into the crawlspace. The online specs for that Weil McLain PWTGO4 boiler indicate it had an Energy Star performance rating of 85.0% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). However, I noticed a Combustion Analyses was performed in October of 2020 (see the piece of paper taped to the top of the burner) that indicates it was operating at 82% efficiency - so I used 82% for my analyses.



In addition to the main boiler, there is also a wood stove on the upper floor at the back (this has a dedicated outside combustion air supply) and there used to be a pellet stove on the upper floor section at the front above the garage. You have removed the pellet stove and are currently having a direct vent propane fireplace installed in that area above the garage. The wood stove is an "optional use" system and the boiler can heat that section of the house without issue. However, you have indicated the boiler heating design for the living area above the garage is not meeting your comfort needs ........... so you definitely want the propane fireplace for supplemental heat to ensure comfortable enjoyment of that area. We talked about a few options, however they would require some "redesigning of the boiler heat distribution to that area:



1. Install some perimeter hydronic floor radiators to obtain better convective air currents for heating (will require some perimeter opening of the floor of the garage ceiling below)

2. Re-configure the heating to full in-floor heat loops (would require taking down the complete garage ceiling)

3. Maybe install a smart thermostat that can incorporate wireless temp sensors that you can place out in the living area itself, rather than just reading the temp on the wall that is way over near the middle of the house. A sample smart thermostat that can incorporate remote sensors is the EcoBee. This solution is the least destructive and will operate the heat loop for that area above the garage longer until the remote sensors read the "comfort temperature" that you have it set to.



For the main oil boiler, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 5" chimney or the 5" combustion air supply pipe. It will not be susceptible to back-drafting combustion fumes back into the house - see my Additional Comments section of this report, specifically on the "Depressurization Test" and "Air Tightness &amp; Ventilation". 



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,661 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 67 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets that are older units that use a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption models. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, the 3 bathroom fans that operate together as a central exhaust ventilation system, and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 18.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil boiler does draw its' combustion air from inside the house (ie: not direct vent). Even though there is a 5" open combustion air supply pipe in the basement to supply the boiler, the depressurization that can be created inside your home could complete with the boiler chimney (potentially drawing combustion products back into the house).



My pressure gauges were installed on the main floor for this test, however as the fancoil ductwork that supplies the main floor also has various duct openings into the basement ....... I am assuming the same negative pressure upstairs is also being created in the basement where the boiler is located.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors on all levels of your home, including 2 in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



NOTE: Please see the "Air Tightness &amp; Ventilation" section below &amp; my "Heating System" section outlined previously in this report - as all of this information is interconnected.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Air Tightness &amp; Ventilation



Let me start off by saying that your house was constructed with a very good building envelope that is quite air tight and has very few (and very minor) air leakage locations. The blower door test I conducted indicated your house has an air tightness of 1.55 Air Changes Per Hour at a negative pressure difference of 50 Pascals (1.55 ACH50). The current air tightness target of the City of Yellowknife for new house construction requires a builder to achieve a target of 1.5 ACH50, so your house from 22 years ago is as tight as any new house hopes to be today !!



As we discussed, building science strives to "Build Tight &amp; Ventilate Right". Now, we'll talk about your house ventilation.



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 x bathroom fans (tied together through a central exhaust ventilation system)

        - The clothes dryer



In addition to the above, the fancoil system (with a zoned heat loop from the boiler) heats a portion of your house and the fancoil includes a "fresh air supply pipe" from the outside of the house. This system is meant to draw in some outside fresh air (mixing it with the return air supply from the ductwork inside the house) and heating the combined air for distribution back into the house. Whenever one or more of the exhaust fan systems (listed above) is turned on, the house requires "makeup air" to replace what is being exhausted .......... so some "makeup air" will be drawn into the house via the same fresh air supply pipe to the fancoil. With the amount of depressurization you can create in your house, I suspect additional "makeup air" is being sucked in through the 5" combustion air pipe for the boiler &amp; perhaps even some coming down the chimney itself (most probably when all the exhaust fans are operating simultaneously).



The type of combination mechanical exhaust (with passive fresh air replacement/supply) was a common design in Yellowknife at the time your house was constructed and the design followed the Building Code requirements of that time. More current houses being built today will have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) centrally installed for the house that will recover about 70% - 80% of the heat from the outgoing stake air &amp; transfer that heat to the outside fresh air coming in. These HRV's are typically installed with their own dedicated low volume ductwork system (separate from the heating supplies) and will incorporate the bathroom exhausts as a "boost" to the HRV for about 5 - 20 minutes.



Overall for your house, the current system seems to be working OK - there are no indications of excessive moisture buildup on windows and the house smells good on all levels. If anything, perhaps the house is a little over-ventilated, but that is better than being under-ventilated. If you ever find that the house is just too dry inside, you could try installing an inline damper on the outside fresh air supply pipe to the fancoil so you can manually adjust it to be partially closed. Of course ......... this will likely result in a bit higher depressurization being created in your house when exhaust fans are being used. For this reason, I am recommending you consider upgrading your boiler to a new Energy Star high efficiency unit that is direct vent, where combustion spillage is not a concern (see the Heating System section of my report).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
